INT. GRAIN ELEVATOR, NIGHT SHIFT - NIGHT

A cathedral of concrete silos. Dust hangs in the lamplight like pollen. Belts clank. Somewhere deep in the dark, grain pours with a sound like breathing.

ODELIA FINCH, 30s, hard hat, headlamp, clipboard, stands on a steel catwalk twenty feet up. Below her, BIG ROY MALLORY, 50s, bearded, sleeves rolled, rakes a heap of wheat from a hopper. And in the doorway of the control shed, DESMOND "DEZ" PARRISH, 20s, a new hire, grips a thermos like a rosary.

ODELIA
Roy, bin four is reading empty.

BIG ROY
Bin four's full. I filled it myself at six.

ODELIA
The gauge says empty.

BIG ROY
Gauge is lying.

DEZ
Miss Finch, I heard something in four.

ODELIA
What kind of something, Dez?

DEZ
A knocking.

BIG ROY
It's the wheat settling, kid.

DEZ
It was knocking back.

Silence. The grain pours in the dark. Odelia's headlamp swings across the silo hatch. Big Roy leans on the rake and does not smile.

BIG ROY
Odelia. Don't open the hatch.

ODELIA
Roy, why not?

BIG ROY
Because I didn't fill it at six.

He sets the rake against the hopper. His hands are shaking. Odelia climbs down the ladder, rung by rung, and crosses to the hatch.

DEZ
Miss Finch. Please.

ODELIA
Dez, go home.

DEZ
The truck's at the other end of the lot.

ODELIA
Then run.

He doesn't. Odelia grips the wheel on the hatch. From inside the silo comes three slow knocks. She drops her hand. Big Roy lets out a breath that is half a sob.

BIG ROY
It's been knocking since June.

ODELIA
Why didn't you say?

BIG ROY
Because nobody asked it what it wanted.

The hatch wheel turns on its own. Slowly. One quarter turn. The headlamp flickers and goes out.
